WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.797 --> 00:00:07.880
JESSICA EARLEY-CHA: Hello.

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:09.990
Ooh, nice and loud, thank you.

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:12.900
Hi, I'm Jessica Dene Earley-Cha,
and I'm a Developer Advocate

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.910
for Actions on Google.

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:15.425
Welcome.

00:00:15.425 --> 00:00:17.550
I'm really excited that
you're all here, especially

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:18.840
because it's the
end of a long day.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.048
So thank you for trucking
along and keeping it going.

00:00:21.048 --> 00:00:22.890
So hopefully, I
have enough energy

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:25.196
if you don't to feed off on.

00:00:25.196 --> 00:00:27.570
And then, what's nice is after
this, we go to happy hour,

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:29.370
so we'll have a good time there.

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:31.130
Yeah.

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:33.070
Oh, well, thank you.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:36.030
So just in case, just
to make sure everyone

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:37.800
knows they're in
the right spot, this

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:41.430
is the Intro to Building
Serverless Apps for Google Home

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:42.600
and Google Assistant.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.727
So if you are not interested
in this, don't worry,

00:00:45.727 --> 00:00:46.560
you could sneak out.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:47.490
That's A-OK by me.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:52.830
Before we really start
and dive into how

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:55.740
to build, let's talk about
a little bit of the user

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:59.700
perspective and let's just
ground ourselves there first.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.740
I really want to
start off with there's

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:03.640
a shift that's going
on right now in regard

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:07.080
to how users are
interacting with technology,

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:10.080
and this happens
every couple of years.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.270
For the sake of
this talk, we just

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:15.160
said, every 10 years or
so there's a major shift.

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:16.680
One of the last
major shifts have

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:18.810
been when we went mobile first.

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:22.500
And I remember being in college
and having my flip phone

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.780
and not understanding
why would I want

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:26.610
to have internet on my phone.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:29.550
That just seemed really
weird and strange.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:31.770
Now in 2018, it's like,
oh my gosh, of course,

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:32.650
I live with my phone.

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:34.020
My phone takes me everywhere.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:37.290
My phone organizes my life.

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:40.110
And so that shift, I
definitely lived through

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:41.380
and I recognize that.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:43.260
And so right now, we're
in a different shift

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:47.010
when it comes to artificial
intelligence and assistance.

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:50.430
And so the way users are
interacting with technology

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:55.116
is definitely different, and
this comes Google Assistant.

00:01:55.116 --> 00:01:56.490
Google Assistant
is where we want

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:59.700
to be able to give users a
way to interact with Google

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.340
where they can be more
productive, get things done,

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.200
and to enjoy life a lot more.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.806
So you can say things like,
OK Google, turn on some music.

00:02:07.806 --> 00:02:09.930
I'm going to try to refrain
from saying that phrase

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:12.240
because I don't want to
trigger anyone's phone.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:14.670
So you could say that
phrase and get things done,

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:16.860
turn on the lights,
do this, do that,

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:21.400
be able to make it
easier for our users.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:25.080
So Google Assistant, right now
we have over a million actions

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:26.610
on the platform.

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:29.850
We're also on over
500 million devices.

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:32.340
And so this number is
really, really amazing,

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:35.490
because Google Assistant
is kind of everywhere now.

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:37.580
We're not just on phones,
but we're on laptops,

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:40.300
we're on [? Korean ?]
cars, we're on watches.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:43.470
And the way we can
interact with users

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:46.080
is everywhere, because
we want to be supportive.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:51.550
Also, we're in lots of different
languages and locales as well.

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:54.810
So let's break this apart in
regards to what, as developers,

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:57.120
can we do with Google Assistant.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.790
So there's the actual
physical device, which there's

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:02.450
over 500 million devices.

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:06.097
And so that is your Google
Homes, your Google Minis.

00:03:06.097 --> 00:03:06.930
This is the machine.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:08.970
This is the hardware.

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:10.680
Next, there is Google Assistant.

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:13.290
This is Google's product where
it's supportive, it's helpful,

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:15.290
it's fantastic.

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:17.267
Then there's Actions on Google.

00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:19.350
And that's a platform that
developers could really

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:23.450
shine and be able to build
really interesting things.

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:25.610
So Actions on Google
is just our platform.

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:27.650
Similar to other
platforms, like Android,

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:29.180
we have developers
who build what

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:30.950
we call "actions"
on the platform

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:35.580
to give users a better and
more rich experience as well.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:37.490
So for the sake
of this talk, I'm

00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:39.130
going to pretend I
have a bike shop.

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:43.130
So I own a business, where
it's a friendly local business.

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:47.540
And I have always wanted
to be a very healthy person

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:48.930
in this alternate universe.

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:51.620
I believe there's a Jessica
there that is really active

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:53.280
and she rides bikes.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:55.190
Unfortunately, that's not me.

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:58.070
So I can definitely
invite you to come

00:03:58.070 --> 00:03:59.480
into my little
world I've created

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:02.060
where I own a bike shop, and
this is kind of my world.

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:03.970
This is an alternate
life I have.

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:05.480
And so in my bike
shop, I want to be

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:08.600
able to provide my users some
really great interactions

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:11.810
and just to be there for
them when they need me most.

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:14.630
And so there's different
moments and times

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:16.370
where users might
want to interact

00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:18.890
with my actual business.

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:22.070
And so I was thinking about,
what kind of interactions

00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:24.680
would someone want
to use if they're

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:26.510
thinking about a bike shop?

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:29.240
And so the first thing I
thought of was the hours.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:30.470
That's pretty common.

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:33.440
I want to be able to give
the ability to my users

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:35.120
to ask, what are your hours?

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:37.550
But I want to go one step
further where it's not just

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:41.330
so much what your hours are,
but if someone's on their bike

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:44.390
and it's broken, they want to
know, can I come in right now?

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:45.800
That's really the question.

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.064
And so I want to provide
that functionality as well,

00:04:48.064 --> 00:04:49.730
where they can ask
me, are you open now?

00:04:49.730 --> 00:04:52.279
And I can actually find
out-- "hey, where are you?

00:04:52.279 --> 00:04:53.820
Can we figure this
out?"-- be able to

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:56.670
give you that attraction,
give you that response.

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:59.660
And then I also thought,
I have a point of sales,

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:03.570
and in there, I actually use a
Firebase to store all my data.

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:07.370
And so I have this
ability to be able to know

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:08.870
what I have in
stock, because I want

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:11.450
to be able to give my users
that type of response.

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:13.940
Because I know I hate
when I go to the store

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:16.340
and they don't have the
product I'm looking for.

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:17.730
That's really
infuriating for me.

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:20.120
So I want to be able to
give my users the ability

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:21.770
to be able to
figure out, do they

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:24.902
want to come in and do I have
the things that they want?

00:05:24.902 --> 00:05:26.360
So we're going to
build that today.

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:31.730
This is a very
small project just

00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:33.740
to illustrate the
things they could do,

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:37.410
and we're going to
build this from scratch.

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:40.960
So high level, this is kind
of what we're working with.

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:43.400
On the left-hand side, the
user could say something

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:46.520
like, OK Google, talk
to the Bike Shop Demo.

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:49.220
The device hears
it, and what it does

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:51.760
is it sends the audio
to Google Assistant.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:53.730
Google Assistant
will parse it out.

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:55.730
We'll use a natural
language processor.

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:58.730
We use our Knowledge Graph,
our machine learning ranking,

00:05:58.730 --> 00:06:02.120
all the jazz to figure out
which action is the best

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:03.890
action that the user wants.

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:05.640
This is really, really similar--

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:07.980
so when you go to the
physical Google.com

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:10.040
and you put something
into the search bar,

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:12.360
it's really similar to
that type of technology.

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:14.320
So Google Assistant
does that, figures out,

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:17.150
"OK, there is a Bike
Shop Demo that exists.

00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:18.490
Let me trigger that."

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:21.560
And Google Assistant will
tell the user, sure, here's

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:22.780
the Bike Shop Demo.

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:25.010
And then now, my action
that I'm building now

00:06:25.010 --> 00:06:27.332
will be able to greet my user.

00:06:27.332 --> 00:06:29.540
My Action will say something
to the effect of "hello,

00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:30.740
welcome to The Bike Shop.

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:32.990
How can I help you?"

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:36.050
After that, the user will
be able to respond back

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:38.030
asking for what the hours are.

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:40.100
That, again, goes
to Google Assistant,

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:43.427
and it should give a response of
"I'm open from 9:00 AM to 5:00

00:06:43.427 --> 00:06:44.510
PM Monday through Friday."

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:45.350
So that's the goal.

00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:47.266
That's kind of where
we're going and these are

00:06:47.266 --> 00:06:49.320
the players we're working with.

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:50.960
So first things
first, let's actually

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:53.800
start a project on
Actions on Google.

00:06:57.860 --> 00:06:58.950
There we go.

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:00.810
And the power of
technology, I'm actually

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:02.004
going to be playing a video.

00:07:02.004 --> 00:07:03.420
That way we don't
mess anything up

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:05.170
and I don't have to
worry about my typing.

00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:07.440
So I'm at the Actions
on Google console.

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:10.920
I've just created an Action.

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:14.340
It drops me into this
really nice platform,

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:19.020
or this console, where it has
on the left-hand side Setup,

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:21.407
Build, Test and
deploy, and Measure.

00:07:21.407 --> 00:07:23.490
And so this helps guide
us through our development

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:24.627
of our Action.

00:07:24.627 --> 00:07:26.460
We're going to skip the
setup part because I

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:28.168
want to go straight
to being able to code

00:07:28.168 --> 00:07:30.430
and making sure that
we finish this in time.

00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:33.155
So what I'm going to do is
I'm going to go to Build

00:07:33.155 --> 00:07:34.530
and I'm going to
go to Action, so

00:07:34.530 --> 00:07:38.530
I can actually build the
functionality I want.

00:07:38.530 --> 00:07:41.710
And so here is where I have
a question that I'm not

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:43.210
really sure how
I want to handle,

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:44.543
and we have a couple of options.

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:49.750
When it comes to
user's requests,

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:52.720
I know for me,
sometimes when I'm

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:56.020
speaking I don't speak
the best grammar--

00:07:56.020 --> 00:07:58.210
like that sentence--
but generally,

00:07:58.210 --> 00:07:59.682
people can understand me.

00:07:59.682 --> 00:08:01.390
And I know there's
going to be users that

00:08:01.390 --> 00:08:03.264
are similar to me who
they might say things

00:08:03.264 --> 00:08:04.930
a little differently,
or a little wonky,

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:07.013
but I want to be able to
process their information

00:08:07.013 --> 00:08:09.580
and give them the correct
feedback or correct responses

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:10.760
that they want.

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:13.660
So I was thinking
about a scenario

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:15.520
that I had drawn up in my head.

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:17.620
And I could definitely see
myself saying something

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:19.240
like this.

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:20.830
"I got this bike last year.

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:22.870
On Friday, I got a flat.

00:08:22.870 --> 00:08:25.800
Can I come in right now?"

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:28.285
And of course, for us as
humans, we could parse that out

00:08:28.285 --> 00:08:30.410
and figure out OK, what
the person is really asking

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:32.000
is if they can
come in right now.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:34.039
But you could imagine
trying to write

00:08:34.039 --> 00:08:36.980
a regular expression trying to
pull out the dates and times

00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:39.250
and the sense of "Friday,"
and "last year," and "now,"

00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:40.720
and trying to figure that out.

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:42.809
[INAUDIBLE] could
be a little messy.

00:08:42.809 --> 00:08:44.120
And I don't necessarily want
to have to deal with all that.

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:45.869
I want to be able to
have something that's

00:08:45.869 --> 00:08:48.320
a little easier of a tool.

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:51.080
And that's when
Dialogflow comes in.

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:55.670
Dialogflow is an intent matching
and entity extraction tool

00:08:55.670 --> 00:08:56.780
that we could use.

00:08:56.780 --> 00:08:59.632
It connects really nicely
with Actions on Google.

00:08:59.632 --> 00:09:01.340
And what is going to
happen is Dialogflow

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:03.680
is going to do a lot of
the heavy lifting for us.

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:08.280
It's going to grab the raw
text that the user says, tries

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:10.970
to figure out the intent
with the intent matching,

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:13.280
and pulls out any
of the entities

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:15.740
that we've predefined that are
important that we would like

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:17.900
to extract from the phrases.

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:21.300
So with Dialogflow,
this is their console,

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:22.730
just to give you a sneak peek.

00:09:22.730 --> 00:09:24.650
We're going to be
using this tool

00:09:24.650 --> 00:09:27.900
with our Actions on Google.

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:29.550
So let's update our
diagram now, now

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:31.130
that things have
changed a little.

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:33.440
So we've added Dialogflow
to our diagram.

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:35.660
And now when Actions
on Google gets--

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:37.530
when it first starts,
it gets triggered,

00:09:37.530 --> 00:09:40.730
it's going to trigger Dialogflow
and the welcome intent.

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:43.850
And so now Dialogflow is going
to be handling that response

00:09:43.850 --> 00:09:46.860
of the greeting that we have.

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:50.750
Likewise, also, every time
that the user speaks to us,

00:09:50.750 --> 00:09:53.270
Google Assistant will
do the whole audio

00:09:53.270 --> 00:09:57.500
to text transformation and then
sends the text to Dialogflow.

00:09:57.500 --> 00:10:00.530
And Dialogflow will handle the
natural language processing

00:10:00.530 --> 00:10:04.494
in regards to that response.

00:10:04.494 --> 00:10:05.490
Cool.

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:07.770
So now we have that.

00:10:07.770 --> 00:10:09.180
So let's actually
build this out.

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:11.510
So let's start on
Actions on Google,

00:10:11.510 --> 00:10:15.110
let's connect all
these together.

00:10:15.110 --> 00:10:18.300
So from here, from the console,
we're going to add an action,

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:20.960
build a custom action.

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:24.470
And this drops us
right into Dialogflow.

00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:26.150
We're going to
create a new agent.

00:10:26.150 --> 00:10:28.965
So that's a new
agent for Dialogflow.

00:10:28.965 --> 00:10:29.840
It will be connected.

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:32.170
And we're going
to go to Intents.

00:10:32.170 --> 00:10:34.940
Dialogflow, by default,
gives us two intents.

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:36.140
First one is Welcome.

00:10:36.140 --> 00:10:38.180
So let's modify Welcome.

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:41.120
As you could see, it already has
a sense of events and training

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:42.370
phrases.

00:10:42.370 --> 00:10:44.260
And training phrases
is what Dialogflow

00:10:44.260 --> 00:10:47.060
will use with its machine
learning to figure out

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:48.760
the intents components.

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:51.320
And so, by default,
Dialogflow gives us

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:53.750
a lot of great examples
for training phrases,

00:10:53.750 --> 00:10:56.900
different ways of saying "hello"
and starting the conversation.

00:10:56.900 --> 00:10:58.970
So we'll have all those.

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:00.380
Those are great.

00:11:00.380 --> 00:11:03.680
And now here with my responses,
I want to make some edits

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:05.030
and make it more customized.

00:11:05.030 --> 00:11:06.470
And so I delete one.

00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:07.880
So I want three.

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:11.435
And this gives us the option of
having three different options

00:11:11.435 --> 00:11:13.320
that our user could hear.

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:15.320
So Dialogflow, whenever
it gets triggered,

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:17.384
will just do a random
from these three options.

00:11:17.384 --> 00:11:19.550
So I'm just going to edit
this text so they can have

00:11:19.550 --> 00:11:23.150
the welcome to The Bike Shop.

00:11:23.150 --> 00:11:25.430
After that, we're
going to hit Save.

00:11:25.430 --> 00:11:27.020
On the bottom
right-hand corner, you

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:30.050
see that the intent gets saved
and that the agent's training

00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:31.310
actually starts.

00:11:31.310 --> 00:11:33.230
And so it's in real
time building out

00:11:33.230 --> 00:11:35.630
the machine learning model.

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:36.490
Builds that out.

00:11:36.490 --> 00:11:39.219
And on the top right-hand
corner, I could type in "hi"

00:11:39.219 --> 00:11:40.760
and we get the
response back already.

00:11:43.650 --> 00:11:46.460
If you guys can't see, up here,
that's where I typed in "hi."

00:11:46.460 --> 00:11:48.132
The user was able to get that.

00:11:48.132 --> 00:11:50.090
And then this is our
response that we get back.

00:11:50.090 --> 00:11:53.226
So in Dialogflow, we
can test this out.

00:11:53.226 --> 00:11:55.100
But let's test this out
on the actual console

00:11:55.100 --> 00:11:56.100
for Actions on Google.

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:59.070
So we go to Integrations,
Integration Settings,

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:01.220
and we're going
to click on Test.

00:12:01.220 --> 00:12:02.220
We'll do some Continue.

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:04.660
It's going to ask to make
sure everything's good.

00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:07.710
It's connecting everything,
which is awesome.

00:12:07.710 --> 00:12:10.010
And now we're dropped
into the simulator.

00:12:10.010 --> 00:12:12.309
This is where, in
Actions on Google,

00:12:12.309 --> 00:12:14.350
you could test out your
actions before you deploy

00:12:14.350 --> 00:12:15.680
and you take it out.

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:19.180
And so in here, right
in the middle part,

00:12:19.180 --> 00:12:21.010
right here is the simulator.

00:12:21.010 --> 00:12:24.880
On the right-hand side is
different options where

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:27.820
it shows the display, what
the users will be seeing,

00:12:27.820 --> 00:12:31.120
as well as the requests and
response, and any bugs as well,

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:32.380
or errors, that shows up.

00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:33.850
And you could see the logs, too.

00:12:33.850 --> 00:12:35.180
So really great tool.

00:12:35.180 --> 00:12:41.140
So we're going to do Talk
to my test app in here.

00:12:41.140 --> 00:12:42.310
Fantastic.

00:12:42.310 --> 00:12:46.600
So our Actions on Google
gave us back the "Good day.

00:12:46.600 --> 00:12:48.190
Welcome to The Bike Shop.

00:12:48.190 --> 00:12:50.140
What can I do for you today?"

00:12:50.140 --> 00:12:55.580
Which worked out wonderfully,
so that worked out.

00:12:55.580 --> 00:12:58.700
So now let's build
out this next intent.

00:12:58.700 --> 00:13:00.940
And let me just elaborate
more on what intents are.

00:13:00.940 --> 00:13:04.890
Intents are the functionality
we want to give to our users.

00:13:04.890 --> 00:13:08.060
This is an option that a
user can say something,

00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:11.720
so then this action can happen,
this part of my bigger action

00:13:11.720 --> 00:13:12.930
can be invoked.

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:19.560
And so we're going to build out
the currently open possibility.

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:22.760
So I'm going to go
back to my video.

00:13:22.760 --> 00:13:25.310
So we're going to go back into
Dialogflow, to that console,

00:13:25.310 --> 00:13:27.350
bring out of here.

00:13:27.350 --> 00:13:29.510
Go back to Intents.

00:13:29.510 --> 00:13:31.780
And we're going to
create a new intent.

00:13:31.780 --> 00:13:35.000
So in here, we're going
to name our intents--

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:37.370
oh, I'm sorry, we're actually
going to build our hours.

00:13:37.370 --> 00:13:38.270
Apologies.

00:13:38.270 --> 00:13:38.930
I got excited.

00:13:38.930 --> 00:13:41.144
I'm going to go
back to my slides.

00:13:41.144 --> 00:13:42.560
We're going to
build this guy out.

00:13:42.560 --> 00:13:43.399
So sorry.

00:13:43.399 --> 00:13:45.440
I'm really excited about
this, as you could tell.

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:47.670
This is really, really awesome.

00:13:47.670 --> 00:13:50.630
So we're going to build our
hours, still in the same space.

00:13:50.630 --> 00:13:53.345
Here, we're actually going to be
adding in our training phrases.

00:13:53.345 --> 00:13:54.720
So again, this is
what Dialogflow

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:58.640
is going to be using to be
able to figure out the intent

00:13:58.640 --> 00:13:59.625
matching.

00:13:59.625 --> 00:14:01.250
And so with this
verbiage that we have,

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:04.220
we want to have as many
examples of training phrases

00:14:04.220 --> 00:14:06.170
that users could possibly say.

00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:08.900
This is a really great
time to get your UX testing

00:14:08.900 --> 00:14:10.950
folks to get involved.

00:14:10.950 --> 00:14:14.220
And if you have designers,
when it comes to conversation,

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:15.950
get them involved too.

00:14:15.950 --> 00:14:18.950
If you don't, great thing to do
is just ask people around you,

00:14:18.950 --> 00:14:20.510
how would you say this?

00:14:20.510 --> 00:14:22.290
And be able to track that.

00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:24.350
So in here, we tracked
it, and now we're

00:14:24.350 --> 00:14:25.820
going to add a response.

00:14:25.820 --> 00:14:30.030
So anytime someone asks this
or says these type of phrases,

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:32.570
our Action's going to
respond back with "we're open

00:14:32.570 --> 00:14:33.560
Monday through Friday--

00:14:33.560 --> 00:14:37.314
or "we're open from 9:00 to
5:00, Monday through Friday."

00:14:37.314 --> 00:14:38.480
And we're going to hit Save.

00:14:42.360 --> 00:14:43.830
Fantastic, the
training has already

00:14:43.830 --> 00:14:46.710
happened on the bottom
right-hand corner.

00:14:46.710 --> 00:14:48.060
Great.

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:50.950
Let's go back to our Actions on
Google console and let's test

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:51.450
this out.

00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:54.660
So we're going to
reset the simulator.

00:14:54.660 --> 00:14:58.830
Let's start this by doing
Talk to my test app.

00:14:58.830 --> 00:15:00.070
Cool, that worked.

00:15:00.070 --> 00:15:01.640
Great.

00:15:01.640 --> 00:15:04.360
And now let's type in
"what are your hours?"

00:15:04.360 --> 00:15:05.740
Or "when are you open?"

00:15:08.900 --> 00:15:09.400
Awesome.

00:15:09.400 --> 00:15:09.900
It worked.

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:12.920
So it gave us "we're
open from 9:00 to 5:00,"

00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:15.415
but notice I put
"when are you open?"

00:15:15.415 --> 00:15:17.840
And I actually didn't have
that in my training phrases.

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:20.200
So again, Dialogflow
was able to figure out

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:25.000
that this phrase matched the
closest with the hours intent

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:26.590
that we had.

00:15:26.590 --> 00:15:29.080
Super easy.

00:15:29.080 --> 00:15:33.700
So now we're going to go with
the currentlyOpen intent,

00:15:33.700 --> 00:15:35.920
and so this gives us
the option for the users

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:38.334
to be able to ask us,
are you open right now?

00:15:38.334 --> 00:15:39.875
So let's build this
out, and this one

00:15:39.875 --> 00:15:42.432
is going to be a little
different from the hours one.

00:15:42.432 --> 00:15:44.140
But we're going to go
from the beginning.

00:15:44.140 --> 00:15:46.120
We create a new intent.

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:47.829
We're going to name it.

00:15:47.829 --> 00:15:49.870
I'm going to use camel
case because in my backend

00:15:49.870 --> 00:15:51.328
I'll be using
Javascript, so I want

00:15:51.328 --> 00:15:53.200
to be able to keep
this consistent.

00:15:53.200 --> 00:15:56.030
We're going to add some
training phrases as well.

00:15:56.030 --> 00:16:00.160
So these have to do with
"can I come in now?"

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:01.480
"Are you open now?"

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:03.790
"Are you currently open?"

00:16:03.790 --> 00:16:06.460
Mind you, I'm only using a small
example because of the demo,

00:16:06.460 --> 00:16:10.060
but the more phrases that you
put in, the better Dialogflow

00:16:10.060 --> 00:16:12.560
is going to perform for you.

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:13.300
Cool.

00:16:13.300 --> 00:16:15.005
We're going to go
down to Responses.

00:16:15.005 --> 00:16:19.540
And here, I don't want to
give a default response.

00:16:19.540 --> 00:16:21.730
This is not going
to be truthful.

00:16:21.730 --> 00:16:24.760
I can't just put, "you're
open now," or "yes, we are"

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:26.710
without knowing
where the user is

00:16:26.710 --> 00:16:30.050
and be able to figure out
what time is it of the day.

00:16:30.050 --> 00:16:33.370
So here, I actually
deleted the response.

00:16:33.370 --> 00:16:36.920
And I'm going to go
down to Fulfillment,

00:16:36.920 --> 00:16:38.890
and I'm going to
enable the webhook.

00:16:38.890 --> 00:16:41.470
That way, when this
intent gets called,

00:16:41.470 --> 00:16:44.400
it's going to go
to my fulfillment.

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:47.400
Going to hit Save and test it.

00:16:47.400 --> 00:16:50.334
Normally, I would test it, but
my fulfillment is my backend,

00:16:50.334 --> 00:16:51.750
and we haven't
built that out yet.

00:16:51.750 --> 00:16:54.000
So we have this
possibility, but we

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:55.530
haven't connected all the way.

00:16:55.530 --> 00:16:57.030
So I'm going to
put a pin in that

00:16:57.030 --> 00:16:59.120
and we're going to keep going.

00:16:59.120 --> 00:17:02.820
And we're going to build out
the intent of tire stock.

00:17:02.820 --> 00:17:05.339
This is the idea that
people could ask me,

00:17:05.339 --> 00:17:06.707
"do you have this type of tire?"

00:17:06.707 --> 00:17:08.290
That way, if they're
riding their bike

00:17:08.290 --> 00:17:11.089
and they pull out their phone,
they're talking to my Action

00:17:11.089 --> 00:17:13.950
because their bike just broke,
they could ask me in real time,

00:17:13.950 --> 00:17:15.160
"do you have something?"

00:17:15.160 --> 00:17:17.292
And I want to give that
functionality to them.

00:17:17.292 --> 00:17:18.500
So let's build that part out.

00:17:22.530 --> 00:17:23.160
Fantastic.

00:17:23.160 --> 00:17:25.726
Similarly, I'm going
to create a new intent.

00:17:25.726 --> 00:17:32.000
I'm going to call
this tiresStocked.

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:34.350
And I want to give it
some training phrases.

00:17:34.350 --> 00:17:39.450
So I put in "my mountain
bike," but I kind of

00:17:39.450 --> 00:17:41.460
feel like I'm missing
something here.

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:44.550
I kind of want to be able
to know what type of tire

00:17:44.550 --> 00:17:45.330
I'm using--

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:48.370
or what tire my
user wants to get.

00:17:48.370 --> 00:17:52.505
So if you double
click in Entities

00:17:52.505 --> 00:17:54.260
that exist in Dialogflow.

00:17:54.260 --> 00:17:55.860
So Dialogflow
understands this idea

00:17:55.860 --> 00:18:00.570
of time, measurements,
common names as well.

00:18:00.570 --> 00:18:04.420
And so there's nothing
like a tire type

00:18:04.420 --> 00:18:06.490
when it comes to
entities with Dialogflow.

00:18:06.490 --> 00:18:09.610
That's a special niche
thing that only my company

00:18:09.610 --> 00:18:10.540
or my business does.

00:18:10.540 --> 00:18:14.980
So what am I going to do instead
is I want to make my own.

00:18:14.980 --> 00:18:19.232
So let's cancel this intent
and let's go to Entities.

00:18:19.232 --> 00:18:21.315
In here, I could build out
my own custom entities.

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:26.400
So this entity I'm
going to call tireType.

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:30.230
This is the idea of a tire type.

00:18:30.230 --> 00:18:33.110
And in here, I'm going
to be able to predefine--

00:18:33.110 --> 00:18:36.620
there's this idea of a mountain
tire, which you could also

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:42.480
use the actual measurements
of 27.5 times 2.0,

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:44.054
or cross country.

00:18:44.054 --> 00:18:45.470
And so what's going
to happen here

00:18:45.470 --> 00:18:49.360
is all these synonyms that
I'm providing to Dialogflow,

00:18:49.360 --> 00:18:53.420
if a user uses that, it knows,
oh, the user means mountain.

00:18:53.420 --> 00:18:55.630
And so it's going to
reduce back to "mountain,"

00:18:55.630 --> 00:18:57.140
and in my backend,
I'm only having

00:18:57.140 --> 00:19:00.290
to handle this idea of mountain.

00:19:00.290 --> 00:19:04.640
Similarly with road terrain,
if someone says racing or uses

00:19:04.640 --> 00:19:08.670
the measurement of
700 times 23, it's

00:19:08.670 --> 00:19:11.130
going to just reduce
down to road terrain.

00:19:11.130 --> 00:19:12.890
And so again, my
backend, I only have

00:19:12.890 --> 00:19:15.020
to worry about this one
variable or this one

00:19:15.020 --> 00:19:20.910
possible value for the tire
type entity that we're creating.

00:19:20.910 --> 00:19:22.280
So I've made all those.

00:19:22.280 --> 00:19:23.435
Fantastic.

00:19:23.435 --> 00:19:25.400
I'm going to go
back to my intents,

00:19:25.400 --> 00:19:26.990
and let's build this out again.

00:19:26.990 --> 00:19:30.450
So again, tiresStocked.

00:19:30.450 --> 00:19:32.210
And now here with
the training phrases,

00:19:32.210 --> 00:19:35.760
I'm going to type in something
to the effect of "my mountain

00:19:35.760 --> 00:19:37.566
bike."

00:19:37.566 --> 00:19:40.190
For some reason, "mountain" is
a really challenging word for me

00:19:40.190 --> 00:19:41.030
to spell.

00:19:41.030 --> 00:19:41.880
I know, right?

00:19:41.880 --> 00:19:43.880
You have no idea how many
times I recorded this.

00:19:43.880 --> 00:19:46.910
This is why I didn't want
to have to live-type it.

00:19:46.910 --> 00:19:50.810
So "my mountain bike got a flat.

00:19:50.810 --> 00:19:52.710
Do you have tires?"

00:19:52.710 --> 00:19:54.910
And here, I could click on that.

00:19:54.910 --> 00:19:57.970
And now I could identify
it as a tire type.

00:19:57.970 --> 00:20:00.200
So now Dialogflow
knows, oh, this

00:20:00.200 --> 00:20:02.270
is a thing I need to
keep track of and give

00:20:02.270 --> 00:20:05.180
to the developer on the backend.

00:20:05.180 --> 00:20:07.730
And now when I did the
BMX one, it automatically

00:20:07.730 --> 00:20:08.550
did that for me.

00:20:08.550 --> 00:20:11.155
So now Dialogflow has learned
that this is information

00:20:11.155 --> 00:20:14.030
that I really want to
keep and keep track of.

00:20:14.030 --> 00:20:18.667
Mind you, there are times where
numbers is a system in entity,

00:20:18.667 --> 00:20:20.750
and so I just have to help
Dialogflow a little bit

00:20:20.750 --> 00:20:25.630
by going, nope, this whole
thing is a tire type.

00:20:25.630 --> 00:20:27.820
So we just give it
super easy, super nice.

00:20:27.820 --> 00:20:29.560
I heard someone say, sweet.

00:20:29.560 --> 00:20:31.000
That's awesome.

00:20:31.000 --> 00:20:32.630
Dialogflow is amazing.

00:20:32.630 --> 00:20:34.840
So with this, I'm going to
say that this is required.

00:20:34.840 --> 00:20:37.580
I need this for my
intent to be triggered.

00:20:37.580 --> 00:20:40.960
And if someone just
says, do you have tires?

00:20:40.960 --> 00:20:44.279
I could ask them automatically,
what type of bike?

00:20:44.279 --> 00:20:46.570
That way, I'm not having to
handle that back and forth,

00:20:46.570 --> 00:20:49.010
but Dialogflow will for me.

00:20:49.010 --> 00:20:50.020
Cool.

00:20:50.020 --> 00:20:50.980
Made that.

00:20:50.980 --> 00:20:53.560
Again, I don't want to
do an automatic response.

00:20:53.560 --> 00:20:56.280
I want to be able to figure
this out dynamically.

00:20:56.280 --> 00:21:02.680
So I'm going to enable my
fulfillment and hit Save.

00:21:02.680 --> 00:21:04.820
Cool.

00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:05.450
Fantastic.

00:21:05.450 --> 00:21:09.896
So we have these three intents
built out that we customized.

00:21:09.896 --> 00:21:11.270
And now, next
thing we want to do

00:21:11.270 --> 00:21:12.978
is we actually want
to build the webhook.

00:21:12.978 --> 00:21:16.820
We want to build it where after
Dialogflow has been triggered,

00:21:16.820 --> 00:21:19.430
it goes and we can
handle this data.

00:21:19.430 --> 00:21:21.490
Actions on Google, we
have a client library

00:21:21.490 --> 00:21:22.940
written in Node.js.

00:21:22.940 --> 00:21:26.240
And this is just an example
of what the hours intent would

00:21:26.240 --> 00:21:29.420
look like if we didn't
automatically program that in,

00:21:29.420 --> 00:21:31.640
where it was automatically
responded by Dialogflow.

00:21:31.640 --> 00:21:33.680
This is what it would look like.

00:21:33.680 --> 00:21:36.680
What I really enjoy about
this is that you just

00:21:36.680 --> 00:21:39.710
make an instance of
Dialogflow, set it to app,

00:21:39.710 --> 00:21:42.830
and now you could
just do app.intent,

00:21:42.830 --> 00:21:46.280
passing in the title or
the name of your intent

00:21:46.280 --> 00:21:48.530
in Dialogflow, which
for this example

00:21:48.530 --> 00:21:52.400
would be "hours," passes
in the conversation object.

00:21:52.400 --> 00:21:53.780
And then in my
function itself, I

00:21:53.780 --> 00:21:58.370
do conv.ask and then
pass in a string.

00:21:58.370 --> 00:21:59.960
Super, super simple.

00:21:59.960 --> 00:22:02.640
This is kind of the basic
thing that you can do.

00:22:02.640 --> 00:22:05.240
There's a lot more really fun
things that you do in addition.

00:22:05.240 --> 00:22:07.230
We'll get to that later.

00:22:07.230 --> 00:22:08.900
So let's build this out.

00:22:08.900 --> 00:22:13.366
Let's build out the
currentlyOpen intent.

00:22:13.366 --> 00:22:16.112
Cool.

00:22:16.112 --> 00:22:17.110
I forgot to mention.

00:22:17.110 --> 00:22:18.860
I'm going to go back here.

00:22:18.860 --> 00:22:20.950
You never want to start
things from scratch.

00:22:20.950 --> 00:22:24.000
So on our GitHub repo, we
do have a Dialogflow webhook

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:26.900
template for Node.js.

00:22:26.900 --> 00:22:28.820
That will have some
boilerplate code.

00:22:28.820 --> 00:22:30.986
And that's what we're going
to be starting off with.

00:22:32.369 --> 00:22:34.910
So this is the code that we're
going to be starting off with.

00:22:34.910 --> 00:22:37.100
It has, by default,
which is really great,

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:40.850
the Welcome intent that
Dialogflow gives us.

00:22:40.850 --> 00:22:44.460
So here we're going to
just edit that to be

00:22:44.460 --> 00:22:47.300
written for our currentlyOpen.

